WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.900
The tempo puts a clock on every severity.

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.586
Critical findings never get a machine fix;

3
00:00:05.586 --> 00:00:08.962
the run halts and a named
person decides immediately.

4
00:00:08.962 --> 00:00:12.988
High findings get an hour, medium
a day, and low three days.

5
00:00:12.988 --> 00:00:16.563
These windows bound the
response, not the drift itself;

6
00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:20.376
a program with no findings is
more likely blind than clean.

7
00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:21.750
Two things to remember.

8
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.787
Automatic fixes hold only for
classes accepted in advance.

9
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:29.856
And when an outside clock is
shorter, like CISA's vulnerability

10
00:00:29.856 --> 00:00:34.001
timelines or the one-hour incident
notice, the outside clock wins.

11
00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:36.100
So how does leadership know it's working?
